{#
  The page every other layout extends.

  The shell of the andrewshell.org design (decision-16): one wrapper at the
  measure, a big site title and tagline on the front page and a small link home
  everywhere else, and a footer holding the copyright, the colophon, the feed
  and whoever the site is by. There is no navigation in the header — the site
  menu belongs with the bio under an entry — and `data-is-root-path` on the
  wrapper is how the stylesheet tells the front page from every other one.

  Blocks a site can override without copying the whole file:
    title       the contents of <title>
    head        the cards, the icons and the structured data, at the end of
                <head>. Call `super()` from an override that only adds to it.
    alternates  <link rel="alternate"> tags, where the feeds go
    header      the site header
    content     the page body
    footer      the site footer, with the search box
    scripts     scripts at the end of <body>

  One variable crosses between a layout and this file: `bioAuthor`, whoever an
  entry's bio credits. A layout that prints `partials/bio.njk` sets it, and the
  footer here leaves the site menu out because the bio is carrying it.
#}{% import "partials/feeds.njk" as feeds %}
{#- Whether this is the front page, which is the one page the header and the
    stylesheet treat differently. `page.url` is the URL being served rather
    than the document's own permalink, so a static homepage under the Reading
    settings is the root path too. -#}
{% set atRoot = (page.url or url or "/") == "/" %}
{#- What the head says this page is, in one place, because the description
    tags, the Open Graph tags and the Twitter card all say the same thing.

    `metaDescription` is the page's own description, else the entry's summary
    — the very line the feeds publish — else the site's tagline. `metaImage` is
    the picture the front matter names, else the site's avatar; with neither,
    nothing is printed rather than an empty tag. `isEntry` is a rendered post
    or page, which is what Open Graph calls an article: a listing has
    `pagination`, and the posts page is a listing carrying a page's front
    matter, so it is a website like every other listing. -#}
{% set metaDescription = description or summary or site.tagline %}
{% set metaTitle = title or site.title %}
{% set metaUrl = (page.url or "/") | absoluteUrl %}
{% set metaImage = image or site.avatar %}
{% set isEntry = (type == "post" or type == "page") and not pagination %}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ site.language | default("en") }}">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>{% block title %}{% if title %}{{ title }} &middot; {% endif %}{{ site.title }}{% endblock %}</title>
    {% if metaDescription %}
    <meta name="description" content="{{ metaDescription }}">
    {% endif %}
    <link rel="canonical" href="{{ (page.url or "/") | absoluteUrl }}">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ "/theme/style.css" | url }}">
    {% block alternates %}
    {{ feeds.feedLinks("/", site.title) }}
    {{ feeds.commentsFeedLink("/comments/feed/", site.title + " comments") }}
    {% if commentsFeed %}
    {{ feeds.commentsFeedLink(commentsFeed, "Comments on: " + title) }}
    {% endif %}
    {% if activityStreams %}
    <link rel="alternate" type="application/activity+json" href="{{ activityStreams }}">
    {% endif %}
    {% endblock %}
    {% if webmention %}
    <link rel="webmention" href="{{ webmention | url }}">
    {% endif %}
    {% block head %}
    {#
      What a link to this page looks like when somebody posts it: the same
      title, description and picture to Open Graph and to a Twitter card,
      which is the pair every reader of this markup understands. The card is
      always `summary` — a small square picture beside the words — because the
      picture is usually the site's avatar rather than a wide photograph.
    #}
    <meta property="og:title" content="{{ metaTitle }}">
    {% if metaDescription %}
    <meta property="og:description" content="{{ metaDescription }}">
    {% endif %}
    <meta property="og:url" content="{{ metaUrl }}">
    <meta property="og:type" content="{% if isEntry %}article{% else %}website{% endif %}">
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ site.title }}">
    {% if metaImage %}
    <meta property="og:image" content="{{ metaImage | absoluteUrl }}">
    {% endif %}
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary">
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ metaTitle }}">
    {% if metaDescription %}
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ metaDescription }}">
    {% endif %}
    {% if metaImage %}
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="{{ metaImage | absoluteUrl }}">
    {% endif %}
    {#
      The site's icons, derived from its avatar. `icons` is empty — and these
      lines print nothing — until a site has one, so a site with no avatar
      links no icon rather than three that answer 404.
    #}
    {% for icon in icons %}
    <link rel="{{ icon.rel }}" type="image/png" sizes="{{ icon.sizes }}" href="{{ icon.href | url }}">
    {% endfor %}
    {% include "partials/jsonld.njk" %}
    {% endblock %}
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="screen-reader-text" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
    <div class="global-wrapper"{% if atRoot %} data-is-root-path="true"{% endif %}>
      {% block header %}
      <header class="global-header">
        {% if atRoot %}
        <h1 class="main-heading">
          <a href="{{ "/" | url }}">{{ site.title }}</a>
        </h1>
        {% if site.tagline %}
        <p>{{ site.tagline }}</p>
        {% endif %}
        {% else %}
        <a class="header-link-home" href="{{ "/" | url }}">{{ site.title }}</a>
        {% endif %}
      </header>
      {% endblock %}

      <main id="main">
        {% block content %}{% endblock %}
      </main>

      {% block footer %}
      <footer>
        <p>&copy; {{ "now" | date("year") }}{% if site.author %}, {{ site.author }}{% endif %} &middot; Published with Geekity</p>
        {#
          The feed and whoever the site is by, as one horizontal list. The links
          come from the site author's profile (decision-16), so what a reader
          sees and what a consumer of `rel="me"` reads are the same list, and a
          site whose author setting names nobody with an account here simply has
          none. Webrings, badges and anything else particular to one site are
          deliberately not here: they go in a site theme's `footer` override.
        #}
        <ul class="hlist">
          <li><a href="{{ "/feed/" | url }}">RSS</a></li>
          {% if siteAuthor.links %}
          {% for link in siteAuthor.links %}
          <li><a rel="me" href="{{ link.href }}">{{ link.label }}</a></li>
          {% endfor %}
          {% endif %}
        </ul>
        {#
          The site menu, on a page that has no bio to carry it.

          The design has no header navigation: the menu is the horizontal list
          inside the bio (TASK-83), which an entry and an author archive print
          and a listing, a 404 or a layout of a site's own does not. So a
          layout that renders a bio sets `bioAuthor`, and this prints the menu
          only where nothing else did — the menu is on every page, once.
        #}
        {#
          The search box, on every page but the one that already has it at the
          top: `query` is on the context of the search page alone, empty or
          not, so its being defined is what says this is that page.
        #}
        {% if query is not defined %}
        {% include "partials/search-form.njk" %}
        {% endif %}
        {% if menu.length and not bioAuthor %}
        <nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Site">
          <ul class="hlist">
            {% for item in menu %}
            <li><a href="{{ item.url | url }}"{% if item.current %} class="is-current" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ item.label }}</a></li>
            {% endfor %}
          </ul>
        </nav>
        {% endif %}
      </footer>
      {% endblock %}
    </div>

    {% block scripts %}
    {#
      The code highlighter, and only where there is code to highlight.

      The CMS renders a fenced block as `pre > code.language-x` and highlights
      nothing, so that a theme picks its own highlighter and runs it on the
      client (decision-16). This one is highlight.js, self-hosted in
      `static/highlight.js` at a pinned version — nothing is loaded from a CDN.

      The test is the rendered body: a page whose `content` holds a
      `language-` class has a code block on it, and every other page — a
      listing, an entry with only prose — ships no JavaScript at all. `defer`
      so it waits for the markup it works on rather than blocking it.

      A site theme that wants a different highlighter, or none, overrides this
      block; one that wants a different set of languages rebuilds the bundle.
      See the theme README.
    #}
    {% if content and "language-" in content %}
    <script src="{{ "/theme/highlight.js" | url }}" defer></script>
    {% endif %}
    {% endblock %}
  </body>
</html>
